so I think I think in a course like this  assessment is tricky that these are  individual clients and projects and we  can try to standardize maybe the design  and engineering challenge to some extent  but that's not necessarily what we took  as our primary consideration we really  started with the people first and tried  to match people to projects and there's  no doubt there are some projects that  are more technically involved there are  some projects with logistics or more  difficult meeting with the client or  even working with with him or her or  even within the within the team so it is  a tricky kind of thing but I think  having us as instructors very involved  in the class and getting a sense of all  the projects having the mentors really  have a strong understanding of different  projects progress so we had one mentor  for every two teams was really key to  the assessment kind of a high-level we  had different pieces over the course of  the semester so doing this midterm  design review counts as part of the  assessment certainly the final  presentations the documentation is a big  part but we also rely on a mentor  feedback client feedback we met we met  with all the clients at the end of the  semester as well as peer feedback you  know this is a team-based project course  so taking into all of these taking in  all these inputs is what we tried to do  when it came to assessment to really try  to understand what students have learned  and how they performed in the in the  course so for some of these we did  prepare rubric or we had a vague sense  of what we were looking for so for  example for the blog post we would score  it based on  just like how weld did you write it in  grammar and how much information is  there but we're looking for really deep  personal reflection and so that's what  you know we gave distinctiveness points4  i believe and so for example you know  you could have a post just on assistive  dogs and what types of assistive dogs  are there which is great it shows that a  student is learning but another post one  of my favorite posts actually over the  semester was from one of my students who  was kind of struggling with should i go  into assistive technology as a career  when everyone is saying you should do  something that's lucrative but this is  like beneficial but what if you know  beneficial isn't profitable what happens  then and she was really struggling with  like am i a selfish person things like  that answer that kind of very deep press  no growth is is something that I valued  a lot I think end up yeah we all valued  a lot um and yeah there's actually very  little weight put on the final project  we put a lot of weight on how well did  they go about the design process and so  how many prototypes what did I manage to  do when they didn't have the skills to  do a certain project were they able to  go look for resources did they go around  MIT knocking on doors asking for  professors for help asking machine shop  technicians for help and that was that  was also highly valued I'm and then what  else do you think I'm missing  we also take into account when the  clients were so some clients would  changed your mind about what project  they wanted the students to work on over  the course of semester which is really  difficult for the students because they  would make progress on something I  didn't have to you know change the  project direction completely and so we  would try and take that into account as  well like how were the students able to  manage that and so because of these kind  of little things you know we really  didn't put a lot of weight on how the  project turned out and every day was  more about how much effort you planted  it how much did you grow as a person how  well did you manage your clients  expectations and yeah were you able to  satisfy the client given all the  constraints so very very organic which  is why we had to spread out the grading  rubric across lots of little components  and that's why we had to get feedback  from mentors and peers and clients and  have mid semester reviews and final  semester reviews and yeah it was I hope  I explained yeah I think I think there's  a lot of aspects to this one is you know  we really did try to structure the  evaluation as much as possible in terms  of being able to try to grade  objectively and so we did have a decent  number of structured rubrics that were  happy to share in terms of how we  evaluated different components of the  project so everything from the blogger  from bump of the course everything from  the blog posts to the videos to the mid  semester final semester panels the  documentation  all aspects of the of the class I think  we for for kind of the individual  components really as what grace says  really trying to personalize the  learning a little bit in terms of having  reflections or blog posts where people  could write about topics that interested  them as it related to assistive  technology I think that's something that  we tried this year as opposed to maybe  more sort of form kind of lab reports on  some of the labs that we did or  something like that the other part I  think we we tried this year was really  to take in as grace is saying all of  these kind of human inputs or feedback  so we met weekly as a staff and kind of  reflected on the projects and students  progress we talked to clients and you  know had students themselves talk a  little bit about their their peers and  so that's I think what we tried to take  into account i think we tried to some  extent we tried to start from what would  be sort of ideal in terms of  understanding students experiences in  the class and that i think makes it a  little bit tricky to evaluate or grade  but hopefully you know it's sort of the  sort of one reasonable way or one you  know valuable way to do it
 

